# DERIVED FROM run-content-profiles.nq, and the diff between them is one
# thing: the sampling. Every profile here says `first-n` where that file says
# `exact`, and the run instant moves so the two are different runs.
#
# It exists for one rule: a count drawn from a sample must never be published
# under `void:entities`, which is a statement about every instance of a class.
# Keeping the rest byte-identical means a test that passes against one file and
# fails against the other has found exactly that, and nothing else.
# REAL fixture, not synthetic, though the ENDPOINT it describes is: a genuine
# --max-cost expensive sweep of tools/synthetic/serve.py, taken 2026-09-05.
# The prober, the queries and the emission are all the shipped ones; only the
# server on the other end is generated, which is what makes the right answers
# knowable.
#
# It exists because no committed fixture carried a content profile. Every other
# run here predates the profile pass, so a test of the explorer had nothing to
# read and the page served a static file instead.
#
# WHAT IT IS SHAPED TO SHOW. The synthetic endpoint declares a DELIBERATELY
# INCOMPLETE vocabulary, so one endpoint produces three of the explorer's four
# states at once:
#
#   verified                 Person, Measurement, ex:name, ex:value
#                            declared in a void partition AND found by a profile
#   undeclared-but-verified  Dataset, Organisation, Record, and the properties
#                            their instances carry: found, never declared
#   declared-only            Ghost, ex:neverUsed: declared and not there
#
# A UI that renders those three the same way is visibly wrong against this file,
# which a fixture where every term agrees could not show.
#
# The counts are known exactly and read back out of the store that built them:
# 47 triples across 3 named graphs and the default graph, 5 classes, 16
# entities. See tools/synthetic/dataset.py.
